<section xml:id="sec-line-integrals">
  <title</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      In this section we study line integrals of scalar functions. We
      develop the idea through a concrete problem: finding the mass of a
      curved wire when we know its density<mdash/>its mass per unit
      length<mdash/>at every point. The strategy is the one behind every
      integral: <em>chop the wire into tiny pieces, multiply the density of
      each piece by its length, and add up the results</em>.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <subsection xml:id="subsec-scalar-line-integrals">
    <title>Line Integrals of Scalar Functions</title>

    <p>
      Suppose we would like to compute the mass of a wire that does not have
      a trivial shape (such as a straight line). If the wire were straight
      and its density constant, the mass would simply be density times
      length. For a curved wire whose density
      <m>\rho(x,y,z)</m> varies from point to point, neither shortcut is
      available<mdash/>but we can recover the same simple rule on small
      pieces.
    </p>

    <p>
      Assume the wire is represented by a curve <m>C</m> in space,
      parametrized by <m>t\in[a,b]</m>, as in
      <xref ref="fig-wire-partition"/>. Divide the wire into <m>n</m> small
      portions, and denote the length of the <m>k</m>th portion by
      <m>\Delta s_k</m>. Pick a sample point <m>(x_k,y_k,z_k)</m> on this
      portion. If the portion is short, the density is nearly constant along
      it, so
    </p>

    <me>
      \text{mass of the } k\text{th portion}
        \approx \rho\left(x_k,y_k,z_k\right)\,\Delta s_k,
    </me>

    <p>
      and summing over all portions,
    </p>

    <me>
      \text{mass of the wire}
        \approx \sum_{k=1}^{n} \rho\left(x_k,y_k,z_k\right)\,\Delta s_k.
    </me>

    <figure xml:id="fig-wire-partition">
      <caption>
        A wire represented by a curve <m>C</m> in space, parametrized by
        <m>t\in[a,b]</m>. The <m>k</m>th portion (red) has length
        <m>\Delta s_k</m> and contains the sample point
        <m>(x_k,y_k,z_k)</m>, located by the position vector
        <m>\mathbf{r}(t_k)</m>.
      </caption>
      <image xml:id="image-wire-partition" width="75%">
        <shortdescription>
          A curve in three-dimensional space with one small portion
          highlighted and a position vector pointing to it from the origin.
        </shortdescription>
        <description>
          <p>
            A three-dimensional coordinate box with x and y running from
            minus 1.3 to 1.3 and z from 0 to 1.7. A thick blue curve
            labelled C rises through the box from a red point labelled t
            equals a, at coordinates (1, 0, 0), to a red point labelled t
            equals b near the top of the box. Partway along the curve a
            short piece is drawn in thick red and labelled Delta s sub k; a
            red point on this piece is labelled (x sub k, y sub k, z sub k).
            A dotted red arrow labelled r of t sub k reaches from the origin
            to that point.
          </p>
        </description>
        <latex-image>
          \begin{tikzpicture}
          \begin{axis}[
              width=11cm, view={115}{18},
              xlabel={\(x\)}, ylabel={\(y\)}, zlabel={\(z\)},
              xmin=-1.3, xmax=1.3, ymin=-1.3, ymax=1.3, zmin=0, zmax=1.7,
              grid=major, grid style={gray!20}, clip=false,
            ]
            % the wire C, before and after the k-th portion
            \addplot3[blue, very thick, domain=0:1.8, samples=80, samples y=0]
                ({cos(deg(x))}, {sin(deg(x))}, {x/2});
            \addplot3[blue, very thick, domain=2.0:3, samples=60, samples y=0]
                ({cos(deg(x))}, {sin(deg(x))}, {x/2});
            % the k-th portion, of length Delta s_k
            \addplot3[red, line width=2.2pt, domain=1.8:2.0, samples=20, samples y=0]
                ({cos(deg(x))}, {sin(deg(x))}, {x/2});
            % position vector r(t_k) from the origin to the sample point
            \addplot3[-&gt;, red, densely dotted, thick] coordinates {(0,0,0) (-0.3233,0.9463,0.95)};
            % endpoints and sample point
            \addplot3[only marks, mark=*, red, mark size=1.5pt] coordinates
                {(1,0,0) (-0.98999,0.14112,1.5) (-0.3233,0.9463,0.95)};
            \node[anchor=north west, xshift=8pt, yshift=-2pt] at (axis cs:1,0,0) {\(t=a\)};
            \node[above left] at (axis cs:-0.98999,0.14112,1.5) {\(t=b\)};
            \node[right=2pt] at (axis cs:-0.3233,0.9463,0.95) {\(\Delta s_k\)};
            \node[left=2pt] at (axis cs:-0.3233,0.9463,0.98) {\((x_k,y_k,z_k)\)};
            \node[right=2pt] at (axis cs:-0.16,0.47,0.475) {\(\mathbf r(t_k)\)};
            \node[blue, right=5pt] at (axis cs:0.878,0.479,0.25) {\(C\)};
          \end{axis}
          \end{tikzpicture}
        </latex-image>
      </image>
    </figure>

    <p>
      This is only an approximation, because the density is not actually
      constant over each portion. How can we make the calculation exact? We
      take the limit in which every portion becomes infinitely small, that
      is, <m>\Delta s_k\to 0</m>, or equivalently <m>n\to\infty</m>:
    </p>

    <me>
      \text{mass of the wire}
        = \lim_{n\to\infty}\sum_{k=1}^{n}
          \rho\left(x_k,y_k,z_k\right)\,\Delta s_k.
    </me>

    <p>
      This limit is denoted by the integral
    </p>

    <me>
      \text{mass of the wire} = \int_C \rho\left(x,y,z\right)\,ds,
    </me>

    <p>
      read <q>the integral of <m>\rho</m> over the curve <m>C</m>.</q> The
      process is animated in <xref ref="fig-wire-partition-video"/>: as the
      partition refines, the sums settle down to the value of the integral.
    </p>

    <figure xml:id="fig-wire-partition-video">
      <caption>
        As the number of portions grows, the sum
        <m>\sum_k \rho(x_k,y_k,z_k)\,\Delta s_k</m> converges to the line
        integral <m>\int_C \rho\,ds</m>.
      </caption>
      <video xml:id="video-wire-partition" width="80%"
             source="videos/wire_mass_partition_1080p.mp4"/>
    </figure>

    <p>
      Our final task is to write the line element <m>ds</m> in terms of
      <m>dt</m>, so the integral becomes an ordinary integral we can
      evaluate. Exactly as in <xref ref="sec-arc-length"/>, the line element
      is the length of an infinitesimal displacement in <m>\R^3</m>,
      <me>ds=\sqrt{(dx)^2+(dy)^2+(dz)^2},</me>
      and factoring out <m>dt</m> gives
    </p>

    <md>
      <mrow>
        \text{mass of the wire} \amp
          = \int_C \rho\left(x(t),y(t),z(t)\right)\,\frac{ds}{dt}\,dt
      </mrow>
      <mrow>
        \amp = \int_C \rho\left(x(t),y(t),z(t)\right)
          \sqrt{\left(\frac{dx}{dt}\right)^2
              + \left(\frac{dy}{dt}\right)^2
              + \left(\frac{dz}{dt}\right)^2}\,dt.
      </mrow>
    </md>

    <p>
      The square root is exactly the length of the velocity vector
    </p>

    <me>
      \mathbf{v}(t)=\mathbf{r}'(t)
        =\left(\frac{dx}{dt}\right)\mathbf{i}
        +\left(\frac{dy}{dt}\right)\mathbf{j}
        +\left(\frac{dz}{dt}\right)\mathbf{k}
        =\left\langle \frac{dx}{dt},\frac{dy}{dt},\frac{dz}{dt}\right\rangle,
    </me>

    <p>
      so the formula takes the tidy form
    </p>

    <me>
      \text{mass of the wire}
        = \int_a^b \rho\left(x(t),y(t),z(t)\right)
          \left\lVert\mathbf{r}'(t)\right\rVert\,dt.
    </me>

    <p>
      There is nothing special about density here. In general, a
      <term>scalar line integral</term> has the form
    </p>

    <me>
      \int_C f\left(x(t),y(t),z(t)\right)\,ds
        = \int_a^b f\left(x(t),y(t),z(t)\right)
          \left\lVert\mathbf{r}'(t)\right\rVert\,dt,
    </me>

    <p>
      where <m>f</m> is a scalar-valued function and the curve <m>C</m>
      specifies the region of integration. Once the curve is parametrized in
      terms of <m>t</m>, the region of integration translates to
      <m>a\le t\le b</m>. In words:
      <em>to compute a line integral, trade the geometric element
      <m>ds</m> for the parameter element
      <m>\lVert\mathbf{r}'(t)\rVert\,dt</m></em>.
      Two sanity checks connect this to what we already know. Taking
      <m>f=1</m> recovers the arc length
      <m>\int_C ds=\int_a^b \lVert\mathbf{r}'(t)\rVert\,dt</m> of
      <xref ref="sec-arc-length"/><mdash/>total length is the
      <q>mass</q> of a wire of density one. And
      <m>\lVert\mathbf{r}'(t)\rVert</m> is the speed
      <m>\left|\mathbf{v}(t)\right|</m> from that section, so the factor
      converts time spent into distance covered.
    </p>
  </subsection>

  <!-- ================================================================= -->
  <!-- EXAMPLE: helical wire. Statement only; solution separate.          -->
  <example xml:id="ex-helix-wire">
    <title>Mass and length of a helical wire</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider a wire of length <m>l</m> and mass <m>m</m>. The wire is
        helix-shaped, with its bottom end located at <m>(1,0,0)</m> and
        parametric equation
        <me>\mathbf{r}(t)=\langle \cos t,\ \sin t,\ t\rangle,\qquad t\ge 0.</me>
        Its density (mass per unit length) is
        <m>\rho(x,y,z)=4x^2y^2</m> kg/m. Assuming that
        <m>m=\dfrac{\pi}{2\sqrt{2}}</m> kg, calculate the length <m>l</m> of
        the wire.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The wire occupies the part of the helix with
        <m>0\le t\le b</m>, where the upper limit <m>b</m> is unknown: it
        marks where the wire ends. Our plan has two steps. First we use the
        known mass in the formula
        <men xml:id="eq-helix-mass">
          m = \int_0^{b} \rho\left(x(t),y(t),z(t)\right)
              \left\lVert\mathbf{r}'(t)\right\rVert\,dt
        </men>
        to find <m>b</m>; then the length is the arc length
        <m>l=\int_0^b \lVert\mathbf{r}'(t)\rVert\,dt</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        First we find <m>\mathbf{r}'(t)</m> and its length:
      </p>
      <md>
        <mrow>
          \mathbf{r}'(t) \amp = \langle -\sin t,\ \cos t,\ 1\rangle,
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \left\lVert\mathbf{r}'(t)\right\rVert \amp
            = \sqrt{\sin^2 t+\cos^2 t+1}=\sqrt{2}.
        </mrow>
      </md>
      <p>
        Next we write the density along the curve in terms of <m>t</m>,
        using the double-angle identity <m>2\sin t\cos t=\sin(2t)</m>:
      </p>
      <me>
        \rho\left(x(t),y(t),z(t)\right)
          = 4x^2y^2
          = 4\cos^2 t\,\sin^2 t
          = \left(2\sin t\cos t\right)^2
          = \sin^2(2t).
      </me>
      <p>
        Substituting into <xref ref="eq-helix-mass"/> and using the
        half-angle identity
        <m>\sin^2(2t)=\tfrac{1}{2}\bigl(1-\cos(4t)\bigr)</m>,
      </p>
      <md>
        <mrow>
          m \amp = \sqrt{2}\int_0^{b}\sin^2(2t)\,dt
             = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}\int_0^{b}\bigl(1-\cos(4t)\bigr)\,dt
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
                 \left(t-\frac{1}{4}\sin(4t)\right)\bigg|_0^{b}
             = \frac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
                 \left(b-\frac{1}{4}\sin(4b)\right).
        </mrow>
      </md>
      <p>
        Setting this equal to the given mass
        <m>m=\dfrac{\pi}{2\sqrt{2}}</m> and multiplying both sides by
        <m>\sqrt{2}</m>,
      </p>
      <me>
        b-\frac{1}{4}\sin(4b)=\frac{\pi}{2}
        \quad\Longrightarrow\quad b=\frac{\pi}{2},
      </me>
      <p>
        since <m>\sin(4\cdot\tfrac{\pi}{2})=\sin(2\pi)=0</m>. (This solution
        is the only one: the left-hand side has derivative
        <m>1-\cos(4b)\ge 0</m>, so it is increasing and crosses the value
        <m>\pi/2</m> exactly once.)
      </p>

      <p>
        The length of the wire is then the arc length from <m>t=0</m> to
        <m>t=\pi/2</m>. Because the speed is the constant <m>\sqrt{2}</m>,
      </p>
      <me>
        l=\int_0^{\pi/2}\left\lVert\mathbf{r}'(t)\right\rVert\,dt
         =\sqrt{2}\int_0^{\pi/2}dt
         =\frac{\pi}{\sqrt{2}}\approx 2.22 \text{ meters}.
      </me>
      <p>
        The wire<mdash/>a quarter turn of the helix from <m>(1,0,0)</m> up
        to <m>(0,1,\pi/2)</m><mdash/>is shown in
        <xref ref="fig-helix-wire"/>, and the accumulation of its mass is
        animated in <xref ref="fig-helix-wire-video"/>.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-helix-wire">
        <caption>
          The helical wire <m>\mathbf{r}(t)=\langle\cos t,\sin t,t\rangle</m>
          for <m>0\le t\le \pi/2</m>, from its bottom end <m>(1,0,0)</m> to
          its top end <m>(0,1,\pi/2)</m>. The dashed gray curve is its
          shadow on the plane <m>z=0</m>.
        </caption>
        <image xml:id="image-helix-wire" width="70%">
          <shortdescription>
            A quarter turn of a helix rising from the point (1,0,0) to the
            point (0,1,pi over 2), with its shadow on the floor.
          </shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>
              A three-dimensional coordinate box with x and y running from
              minus 1.2 to 1.2 and z from 0 to 1.8. A thick blue curve, one
              quarter turn of a helix, starts at a red point on the floor
              labelled (1, 0, 0), t equals 0, and climbs steadily as it
              curves, ending at a red point labelled (0, 1, pi over 2), t
              equals pi over 2, about 1.57 units above the floor. A dashed
              gray quarter circle on the floor of the box shows the curve's
              shadow.
            </p>
          </description>
          <latex-image>
            \begin{tikzpicture}
            \begin{axis}[
                width=10cm, view={115}{20},
                xlabel={\(x\)}, ylabel={\(y\)}, zlabel={\(z\)},
                xmin=-1.2, xmax=1.2, ymin=-1.2, ymax=1.2, zmin=0, zmax=1.8,
                grid=major, grid style={gray!20}, clip=false,
              ]
              % shadow on the ground z=0
              \addplot3[gray!50, dashed, thin, domain=0:90, samples=60, samples y=0]
                  ({cos(x)}, {sin(x)}, {0});
              % the helix-shaped wire
              \addplot3[blue, very thick, domain=0:90, samples=80, samples y=0]
                  ({cos(x)}, {sin(x)}, {pi*x/180});
              % endpoints
              \addplot3[only marks, mark=*, red, mark size=1.6pt] coordinates
                  {(1,0,0) (0,1,1.5708)};
              \node[anchor=south west, xshift=5pt, yshift=2pt] at (axis cs:1,0,0) {\((1,0,0),\ t=0\)};
              \node[above left=1pt] at (axis cs:0,1,1.5708) {\((0,1,\pi/2),\ t=\pi/2\)};
            \end{axis}
            \end{tikzpicture}
          </latex-image>
        </image>
      </figure>

      <figure xml:id="fig-helix-wire-video">
        <caption>
          A point traverses the helical wire while the accumulated mass
          <m>\int_0^t \rho\,\lVert\mathbf{r}'\rVert\,d\tau</m> grows to
          <m>\dfrac{\pi}{2\sqrt{2}}\approx 1.11</m> kg over the length
          <m>\dfrac{\pi}{\sqrt{2}}\approx 2.22</m> m.
        </caption>
        <video xml:id="video-helix-wire" width="85%"
               source="videos/helix_wire_mass_1080p.mp4"/>
      </figure>
    </solution>
  </example>

</section>
